ML Workflow Understanding


	Data Collection: Gather raw data from sources (databases, APIs, sensors).

	Data Preprocessing: Clean, normalize, and transform data for training.

	Feature Engineering: Extract meaningful features to improve model performance.

	Model Training: Apply algorithms to learn patterns from data.

	Evaluation: Measure performance using metrics.

	Deployment: Package and serve the model for inference.

	Monitoring & Maintenance: Track drift, retrain, and update models.


Model Training Lifecycle

	Step 1: Data Preparation → Ensure quality and consistency.

	Step 2: Model Selection → Choose algorithm based on problem type.

	Step 3: Training → Fit model on training data.

	Step 4: Validation → Tune hyperparameters using validation sets.

	Step 5: Testing → Evaluate final model on unseen data.

	Step 6: Logging & Versioning → Track experiments with MLflow/DVC.

	Step 7: Deployment → Register and push model to production.



Inference Pipeline


	Input Handling: Accept raw input (API, batch job, stream).

	Preprocessing: Apply same transformations used during training.

	Feature Extraction: Convert input into model-ready features.

	Model Prediction: Run inference using trained model.

	Postprocessing: Convert predictions into human-readable outputs.

	Serving: Deliver results via API, dashboard, or application.


Feature Engineering Basics

	Scaling & Normalization: Standardize values for consistency.

	Encoding: Convert categorical variables (one-hot, label encoding).

	Dimensionality Reduction: PCA, feature selection to reduce noise.

	Domain Features: Create new features based on domain knowledge.

	Time-based Features: Extract seasonality, trends, lags for temporal data.



Evaluation Metrics

	Classification:

		Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1-score

		ROC-AUC, Confusion Matrix

	Regression:

		Mean Squared Error (MSE), Mean Absolute Error (MAE)

		R² (Coefficient of Determination)

	Clustering:

		Silhouette Score, Davies-Bouldin Index

	Ranking/Recommendation:

		NDCG, MAP, Hit Rate


Supervised Learning

	Definition: Training models on labeled data (input → output pairs).

	Goal: Learn a mapping function to predict outputs for new inputs.

	Examples: Spam detection, house price prediction, sentiment analysis.


Classification vs Regression

	Classification: Predict discrete categories (e.g., spam/not spam, disease/no disease).

	Regression: Predict continuous values (e.g., house price, temperature).


Overfitting

	Definition: Model learns noise instead of general patterns.

	Symptoms: High training accuracy, poor test accuracy.

	Solutions:

		Regularization (L1/L2 penalties).

		Cross-validation.

		Simplify model complexity.

		More training data.


Train/Test Split

	Purpose: Evaluate generalization on unseen data.

	Typical Split: 70–80% training, 20–30% testing.

	Validation Set: Sometimes added for hyperparameter tuning.



Feature Engineering

	Scaling: Standardization, normalization.

	Encoding: One-hot, label encoding for categorical variables.

	Domain Features: Derived from domain knowledge (e.g., ratios, time lags).

	Dimensionality Reduction: PCA, feature selection.


Cross-Validation



	Definition: Splitting data into multiple folds to test robustness.

	Types:

	k-Fold CV

	Stratified k-Fold (for imbalanced classes)

	Leave-One-Out CV

	Benefit: Reduces variance in performance estimates.


Algorithms

	Linear Regression: Predicts continuous values using linear relationships.

	Logistic Regression: Classification using sigmoid function for probabilities.

	Random Forest: Ensemble of decision trees, robust to overfitting.

	XGBoost: Gradient boosting framework, highly efficient and accurate.

Libraries

	NumPy: Numerical computing, arrays, linear algebra.

	Pandas: Data manipulation, tabular structures (DataFrames).

	Scikit-learn: ML toolkit for preprocessing, training, evaluation, pipelines.


